# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

Execution requires the user's approval of this plan.

## Goal
Show how treated and untreated samples separate on a PCA of the gene-level counts.
Use all six samples: three untreated and three treated, as labelled in the resolved design.

## Inputs
Resolve both inputs at execution time using `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`. Paths below are relative to the project and reflect current resolution. Inputs remain read-only.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper; native | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets; native | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. Run Python in `gars-bio`, sourced through the workspace's `_system/gars-env.sh`, using pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib. Record package versions, resolved input paths and SHA256 hashes in the report. Check dependencies in the batch job; if unavailable, stop without installing anything.
2. Load the tab-delimited native gene counts and the CSV design. Use `gene_id` as the gene identifier and exclude `gene_name` from the numeric matrix. Require unique, nonempty gene and sample identifiers, finite nonnegative counts, positive sample library totals, and an exact one-to-one match between count columns and design sample IDs. Reorder columns to the design. Preserve fractional counts. Fail on mismatches rather than silently dropping samples.
3. Normalize each sample by its total count to counts per million (CPM). Keep genes with CPM >= 1 in at least three of the six samples, then transform to log2(CPM + 1). Record library totals and retained gene counts. Use this explicitly exploratory transformation of the supplied length-scaled gene counts; do not perform differential expression or infer treatment causality.
4. Remove genes with zero transformed variance. Select up to 500 genes with the largest sample variance (ddof=1), breaking ties by gene_id. Selection uses all samples without condition labels. Record selected IDs and variances. Require at least two variable genes and two nonzero principal components; otherwise stop with a diagnostic.
5. Transpose to samples by genes, center each gene without scaling to unit variance, and run scikit-learn PCA with `svd_solver="full"`, retaining all available components. Export sample coordinates and explained variance for every component. Plot PC1 versus PC2 with distinct colours for treated and untreated, sample ID labels, and axis labels showing explained variance percentages. Do not remove samples, correct batches, or choose genes based on treatment association.
6. Write a short report documenting the normalization, filtering, selected gene count, sample labels, variance percentages and any visible grouping or outlier pattern in PC1/PC2. Treat visual separation as descriptive; no significance test or mechanistic claim is planned. Save all declared outputs inside this analysis directory, then run `stage03_analysis.py verify` and append its returned history entry verbatim as required by the stage contract.

